\chapter{Mixed models I: why and how}

\section{The problem: not all observations are independent}

Every model so far has assumed each row of your data is a fresh, independent piece
of information. Very often it is not:

\begin{itemize}
  \item the same patient measured at five visits;
  \item 30 pupils in each of 40 schools;
  \item repeated litters from the same mother;
  \item several plots inside each farm, several farms inside each district.
\end{itemize}

Two pupils from the same school are more alike than two pupils picked at random.
So your 1200 pupils do not carry 1200 pupils' worth of information about schools.
Treating them as if they do makes your standard errors far too small.

\begin{plainenglish}[Effective sample size]
If observations within a cluster are correlated, your real sample size is
somewhere between the number of clusters and the number of observations. In the
worst case --- perfectly correlated observations --- 30 measurements on one person
tell you exactly as much as one measurement.
\end{plainenglish}

\section{How bad is it? A simulation}

<<cluster-damage>>=
set.seed(77)
sim_cluster <- function(n_group = 20, n_per = 10, sd_group = 1.2) {
  g   <- rep(1:n_group, each = n_per)
  x   <- rep(rnorm(n_group), each = n_per)      # a GROUP-level predictor
  b_g <- rep(rnorm(n_group, 0, sd_group), each = n_per)   # group random effect
  y   <- 0 + 0 * x + b_g + rnorm(n_group * n_per, 0, 1)   # TRUE effect of x is 0
  d   <- data.frame(y, x, g = factor(g))
  p_lm  <- summary(lm(y ~ x, data = d))$coefficients["x", 4]
  p_lmm <- {
    m <- lme4::lmer(y ~ x + (1 | g), data = d)
    2 * pnorm(abs(summary(m)$coefficients["x", "t value"]), lower.tail = FALSE)
  }
  c(ignoring_clusters = p_lm, mixed_model = p_lmm)
}
ps <- t(replicate(400, sim_cluster()))
round(colMeans(ps < 0.05), 3)          # how often each finds a "significant" effect
@

The true effect of \fn{x} is exactly zero. Ignoring the clustering, we declare
significance \Sexpr{round(100*mean(ps[, 1] < 0.05))}\% of the time instead of 5\%.
The mixed model stays near the advertised rate.

\begin{warningbox}
Clustering hurts most for predictors that vary \emph{between} clusters (treatment
assigned per school, exposure assigned per farm). For predictors that vary
\emph{within} a cluster the damage is usually smaller --- but it is never zero.
\end{warningbox}

\section{Fixed effects versus random effects}

\begin{center}
\small\sffamily
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.20\textwidth}p{0.38\textwidth}p{0.38\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
 & \textbf{Fixed effect} & \textbf{Random effect} \\
\midrule
The levels are & the specific ones you care about & a sample from a larger population \\
You estimate & one coefficient per level & one \emph{variance} \\
Cost in df & $k-1$ parameters & 1 parameter \\
Can predict new levels & no & yes \\
Handles unbalanced data & poorly & well \\
Typical examples & treatment, sex, dose & subject, school, batch, litter, plot \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{keyidea}[The practical test]
Ask: ``if I repeated this study, would I get the same levels of this variable?''
Same treatments, same sexes --- fixed. A different set of schools, patients or
batches --- random.
\end{keyidea}

\section{The random-intercept model}

\[
y_{ij} = \underbrace{\beta_0 + \beta_1 x_{ij}}_{\text{fixed part}}
       + \underbrace{u_j}_{\text{random intercept}} + \varepsilon_{ij},
\qquad u_j \sim N(0, \sigma_u^2), \quad \varepsilon_{ij} \sim N(0, \sigma^2).
\]

Each group $j$ gets its own intercept $\beta_0 + u_j$, and those intercepts are
assumed to be drawn from a normal distribution whose variance we estimate.

<<sleep-fit>>=
library(lme4)
data(sleepstudy)
str(sleepstudy)
m_ri <- lmer(Reaction ~ Days + (1 | Subject), data = sleepstudy)
summary(m_ri)
@

\subsection{Reading the random effects table}

<<varcorr>>=
VarCorr(m_ri)
as.data.frame(VarCorr(m_ri))
@

\begin{plainenglish}
The \fn{Subject} variance is the spread of the subject-specific intercepts: how much
people differ from each other on average. The \fn{Residual} variance is the spread
of measurements within a person. Together they partition the total variability
into ``between people'' and ``within people''.
\end{plainenglish}

\subsection{The intraclass correlation}

\[
\text{ICC} = \frac{\sigma_u^2}{\sigma_u^2 + \sigma^2}
\]
--- the proportion of total variance that is between groups, and equivalently the
correlation between two observations from the same group.

<<icc>>=
vc <- as.data.frame(VarCorr(m_ri))
icc <- vc$vcov[1] / sum(vc$vcov)
round(c(between_subject_var = vc$vcov[1], within_subject_var = vc$vcov[2],
        ICC = icc), 3)
@

An ICC of \Sexpr{round(icc, 2)} means about
\Sexpr{round(100*icc)}\% of the variation in reaction time is between people rather
than within them. Any ICC noticeably above 0 means clustering must be modelled.

\section{Random slopes}

A random intercept says everyone starts at a different level. A random slope says
everyone \emph{responds} differently too.

\[
y_{ij} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_{ij} + u_{0j} + u_{1j}x_{ij} + \varepsilon_{ij}
\]

<<sleep-rs>>=
m_rs <- lmer(Reaction ~ Days + (Days | Subject), data = sleepstudy)
summary(m_rs)$varcor
anova(m_ri, m_rs)
@

The likelihood-ratio test strongly favours the random-slope model: subjects really
do differ in how badly sleep deprivation affects them.

<<slopes-plot, fig.width=9.4, fig.height=4.4, out.width="\\textwidth", fig.cap="Left: each subject's own trajectory with the population average in bold. Right: subject-specific intercepts and slopes from the mixed model. The spread of the points is exactly what the random-effects variances measure.">>=
par(mfrow = c(1, 2)); par_book()
plot(sleepstudy$Days, sleepstudy$Reaction, type = "n",
     xlab = "days of sleep deprivation", ylab = "reaction time (ms)",
     main = "Every subject has their own line")
grid_book()
for (s in unique(sleepstudy$Subject)) {
  d <- subset(sleepstudy, Subject == s)
  cf <- coef(m_rs)$Subject[as.character(s), ]
  abline(cf[[1]], cf[[2]], col = adjustcolor(PAL["grey"], 0.75), lwd = 1.2)
  points(d$Days, d$Reaction, pch = 21, bg = adjustcolor(PAL["primary"], 0.20),
         col = "white", cex = 0.8)
}
abline(fixef(m_rs)[1], fixef(m_rs)[2], lwd = 4, col = PAL["accent"])
legend("topleft", bty = "n", cex = 0.8, lwd = c(4, 1.2),
       col = c(PAL["accent"], PAL["grey"]),
       legend = c("population average", "individual subjects"))

re <- ranef(m_rs)$Subject
plot(re[, 1], re[, 2], pch = 21, bg = adjustcolor(PAL["accent"], 0.6), col = "white",
     cex = 1.6, xlab = "random intercept (deviation, ms)",
     ylab = "random slope (deviation, ms/day)",
     main = "Subject-level deviations")
grid_book(); abline(h = 0, v = 0, lty = 3, col = PAL["grey"])
text(re[, 1], re[, 2], rownames(re), pos = 3, cex = 0.55, col = PAL["grey"])
@

\section{Shrinkage: the best feature of mixed models}

Fit a separate regression to each subject and you get 18 completely independent
estimates, each noisy. The mixed model instead pulls each subject's estimate
towards the overall average --- by a lot if that subject has little data or is
extreme, hardly at all if the subject has plenty of consistent data.

<<shrinkage, fig.width=7.2, fig.height=4.4, out.width="0.82\\textwidth", fig.cap="Arrows run from each subject's own separate regression fit to the mixed model's estimate. Estimates furthest from the centre are pulled in the most: this is shrinkage, and it is what makes mixed-model estimates more accurate on average.">>=
par_book()
indiv <- t(sapply(split(sleepstudy, sleepstudy$Subject),
                  function(d) coef(lm(Reaction ~ Days, data = d))))
mixed <- as.matrix(coef(m_rs)$Subject)
plot(rbind(indiv, mixed), type = "n", xlab = "intercept", ylab = "slope (ms/day)",
     main = "Shrinkage towards the population mean")
grid_book()
arrows(indiv[, 1], indiv[, 2], mixed[, 1], mixed[, 2], length = 0.06,
       col = adjustcolor(PAL["grey"], 0.9), lwd = 1.5)
points(indiv[, 1], indiv[, 2], pch = 21, bg = adjustcolor(PAL["red"], 0.5),
       col = "white", cex = 1.4)
points(mixed[, 1], mixed[, 2], pch = 21, bg = adjustcolor(PAL["green"], 0.7),
       col = "white", cex = 1.4)
points(fixef(m_rs)[1], fixef(m_rs)[2], pch = 4, cex = 2.4, lwd = 3, col = PAL["primary"])
legend("topright", bty = "n", cex = 0.75, pch = c(21, 21, 4),
       pt.bg = c(adjustcolor(PAL["red"], 0.5), adjustcolor(PAL["green"], 0.7), NA),
       col = c("white", "white", PAL["primary"]),
       legend = c("separate lm per subject", "mixed model (shrunk)", "population average"))
@

\begin{keyidea}
Shrinkage is not a bug or a fudge. It is the mathematically optimal way to combine
what one subject tells you with what all the other subjects tell you. It is why a
mixed model beats fitting each group separately, especially when groups are small
or unbalanced.
\end{keyidea}

\section{Specifying random effects in \R}

The formula syntax is compact and worth learning properly.

\begin{center}
\small\sffamily
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.30\textwidth}p{0.66\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Syntax} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
\fn{(1 | g)}            & random intercept per level of \fn{g} \\
\fn{(x | g)}            & random intercept and random slope for \fn{x}, correlated \\
\fn{(0 + x | g)}        & random slope only, no random intercept \\
\fn{(1 | g) + (0 + x | g)} & random intercept and slope, forced uncorrelated \\
\fn{(x || g)}           & shorthand for the line above \\
\fn{(1 | g1/g2)}        & \fn{g2} nested inside \fn{g1}; same as \fn{(1|g1) + (1|g1:g2)} \\
\fn{(1 | g1) + (1 | g2)} & crossed random effects \\
\fn{(1 | obs)}          & observation-level random effect (Chapter 15) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{warningbox}[Nested versus crossed depends on your labels]
If classroom is labelled 1, 2, 3 \emph{within every school}, then class ``1'' in
school A and class ``1'' in school B are different classes and you must write
\fn{(1|school/class)}. If every class has a globally unique ID, \fn{(1|school) +
(1|class)} gives the same fit. Getting this wrong silently pools unrelated groups
together.
\end{warningbox}

\section{REML or ML?}

\fn{lmer} uses REML (restricted maximum likelihood) by default. REML gives less
biased variance estimates; ML gives comparable likelihoods for models with
different fixed effects.

\begin{center}
\small\sffamily
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.42\textwidth}p{0.24\textwidth}p{0.30\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{You want to\dots} & \textbf{Use} & \textbf{\R} \\
\midrule
compare models differing in \emph{fixed} effects & ML & \fn{REML = FALSE} \\
compare models differing in \emph{random} effects & REML (or ML) & default \\
report final variance estimates & REML & default \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

<<reml-ml>>=
a <- lmer(Reaction ~ Days + (Days | Subject), data = sleepstudy, REML = FALSE)
b <- lmer(Reaction ~ 1    + (Days | Subject), data = sleepstudy, REML = FALSE)
anova(b, a)                # comparing fixed effects: both fitted with ML
@

Helpfully, \fn{anova()} on \fn{merMod} objects automatically refits with ML when
you compare models, so this trap is largely handled for you.

\section{Where are the $p$-values?}

\fn{lmer} deliberately prints $t$ values without $p$-values, because the correct
denominator degrees of freedom for an unbalanced mixed model are genuinely not
known. Your options:

<<pvalues>>=
## 1. Likelihood-ratio test (recommended; refit with ML)
anova(b, a)$`Pr(>Chisq)`[2]

## 2. Wald with a normal approximation -- fine when you have many clusters
tv <- summary(m_rs)$coefficients["Days", "t value"]
c(t = tv, p_normal_approx = 2 * pnorm(abs(tv), lower.tail = FALSE))

## 3. Profile-likelihood confidence intervals -- often the most informative
confint(m_rs, method = "profile", oldNames = FALSE)
@

A fourth option is \fn{lmerTest::lmer}, which adds Satterthwaite or
Kenward--Roger degrees of freedom and prints $p$-values in the usual place. With
few clusters (under about 30) Kenward--Roger is the safest choice.

\begin{rulebox}
Many clusters (40+): the normal approximation is fine. \\
Few clusters (under 30): use Kenward--Roger, or bootstrap
(\fn{confint(m, method = "boot")}). \\
Comparing nested models: use the likelihood-ratio test, refitted with ML.
\end{rulebox}
